# A scope-trail segment whose relation is not in the vocabulary.
#
# ux:relation is a CLOSED list -- contains, narrows, source, target. A relation
# outside it is refused by sh:in. That is the constraint worth exercising here:
# an open string would let a segment describe a relationship nothing downstream
# knows how to walk, and it would do so without any error.
#
# The scope is a well-formed IRI and applicable is a real boolean, so the only
# fault is the relation. An example wrong in several ways proves whichever
# check ran first, which is not the same as proving this one.
@prefix ux:  <https://w3id.org/cpcp/osi8/ux#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<https://example.test/scope-trail/bad> a ux:ScopeTrailSegment ;
  ux:scope <https://example.test/scope/checkout> ;
  # INTENTIONALLY OUTSIDE the vocabulary: contains | narrows | source | target
  ux:relation "supersedes" ;
  ux:applicable true .
